      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-276774",
      ":question/problem": "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,{300}^{∘}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,-\\sqrt{3}).$"
